  "code": "REFUTED-mamba-checkpoint-eviction",
  "statement": "The mamba/linear-attention checkpointed-state eviction hypothesis does not survive. (1) Universality is trivial: the append residual equals the appended-tail term on the 64-grid -- residual == L - 64*floor(L_prev/64) - L%64 -- on 31/35 controlled appends (89%), i.e. the reusable prefix is exactly the previous prompt floored to 64 and the new body is simply not cached yet. (2) The remaining deviation is bounded by ONE appended body and is not pressure-driven: in the U4-shaped reproduction (bodies of 27 sentences, ~896 tokens) residual is 896/960 (deficit 0) on 6/10 appends and 1728/1792 (deficit 896 = one body) on 4/10, so U4's median 1792 = body (896) + one-body boundary lag (896). (3) Deliberate checkpoint pressure did not move it: 24 fresh-salt prefixes (~6.5k tokens of distinct radix nodes) inserted between appends left the next append's deficit at 0 (p60 rounds 2-3: 0,0), identical to the unpressed c60 control. (4) The mamba pool was saturated (used 4, available 0-1, evictable 59-60 of 64 slots) and flat for the whole run, yet 31/35 appends lost nothing -- eviction would have to bite permanently, it did not. (5) linear_replayssm_cache_len=16 is inert (enable_linear_replayssm=false), so that path cannot be the cause. What survives is a boundary/insertion lag of at most one appended body (all deficits are exact multiples of 64, <= one body), not eviction.",
